{"kind":"artifact","text":"README.md at b86eeb8b6ac7df32ec174496924e325ddc7c2278 in the gitseq-chess repository, under promise bbb9a922 on request 747adca9.\n\nThe request asked for the --key escape hatch to be documented and for the local threat model behind the secret change to be written down, so the documentation is part of what was requested rather than a courtesy.\n\nWHAT CHANGED. The secret examples no longer pass a value as a flag, because that flag no longer exists; they write the secret to a 0600 file and pass --join-secret-file and --secret-file, and the page says why: a command argument is visible in the process table to every account on the machine for as long as the command runs. It records that naming a source which yields nothing is an error rather than an absent secret, so a mistyped path cannot quietly create an invitation anyone can accept.\n\nA new section says where the player key lives and what protects it: the bounding directory is opened once and every name resolves against it, so a component replaced while the command runs cannot redirect the key; a symbolic link standing in for the key or for the chess directory is refused even when it points back inside the same directory; publication writes beside the destination and links into place so the real name never holds a half-written key; and the mode is set explicitly rather than left to the umask.\n\nThe --key boundary is stated plainly rather than implied, which is the part the request specifically asked for. Choosing the location, and everything above the file's own parent, is the operator's; the program pins that parent and does not inspect the path that led to it, so ancestor links are their responsibility, and the parent must already exist. Then it lists what is still refused wherever the file lives: a final symlink, a non-regular file, a file readable by anyone but its owner, a file whose identity changes between being named and being opened, and any publication that is not exclusive.\n\nI also deleted a sentence that had become false. The old page said custody hardening for caller-selected key paths was \"tracked separately from the chess application semantics in this head\". It is this head.\n\nGATES are those recorded on the cmd/chess artifact 1c052a75 for this same exact head: gofmt, vet, GOOS=windows vet, the full suite, -race, and diff check all clean.","body":{"commit":"b86eeb8b6ac7df32ec174496924e325ddc7c2278","path":"README.md"}}